From 39d0ed2421d89595f5f71467e5005262fe031200 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: electrs-pruned <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 18:38:37 -0500
Subject: [sparrow PATCH] Follow a chain whose block headers are not all 80
 bytes

The client half of docs/electrum-header-v2.md. Needs the drongo patch
beside it, which is what actually reads and hashes a v2 header; this is
everywhere Sparrow assumed a header is 80 bytes.

VariableHeaders holds all of it, so removing support later is a file
deletion plus reverting the call sites that name it.

  - blockchain.block.headers arrives as one concatenated hex string
    below protocol 1.6 and as a list of them at 1.6 and above. Both are
    read now. Three call sites sliced the concatenated form on a fixed
    stride; on a chain spanning the activation a 40 header response is
    4964 bytes, which a fixed stride reads as 62 headers of garbage. The
    walk reads each header's length from its own version field and fails
    if it does not land exactly on the end.

  - checkBlockHeaders enforced that same stride on the whole response,
    one layer above those call sites, so a chain carrying 164 byte
    headers was refused before anything that could read them was
    reached. It now counts the run rather than dividing it, walking the
    hex without decoding it so the check stays as cheap as it was.

  - The list form is not optional given the version bump below. Fulcrum
    2.1.2 caps at 1.6 and answers in that form, so a client offering 1.8
    receives the list where one offering 1.4.2 received the string.
    Reading only the string would break header sync against a Fulcrum
    server on every network, mainnet included.

  - A header marked v2 is refused on a network that cannot carry one, so
    mainnet is exactly as strict as it was before variable lengths
    existed: 80 bytes and nothing else. Regtest joins testnet4 in being
    able to, since a node there activates BLAKE2b with
    -testactivationheight and that is where this is exercised.

  - The on-disk store indexes by height times a stride, so the stride is
    now per network rather than per header and an 80 byte header is
    padded out where both can occur. Only testnet4 and regtest can, so
    every other network is byte-for-byte as before and existing mainnet
    stores are untouched. A store written with the other stride is already handled:
    one whose first record does not descend from the pin is discarded and
    re-downloaded, and a padded record cannot descend from anything.

  - SUPPORTED_VERSIONS reaches 1.8. A server on a chain with v2 headers
    refuses to negotiate below it, so this is what makes such a chain
    reachable at all; the range is a minimum and a maximum, so servers
    elsewhere still negotiate the highest they support at or below it.

Sparrow's checkpoints are not in the way: testnet4 pins 74 periods, so
149183, which is 354 blocks below the activation and on shared history.

BlockHeadersResponseTest takes a response through the path a running
Sparrow uses, from the JSON-RPC call through deserialization and the
checks to the split, in both forms. It uses the real testnet4 headers
149536 and 149537, the last v1 header and the first v2 one, and asserts
both hashes and that 149537 names 149536 as its parent. Restoring the
fixed stride fails it, which is what makes it a regression test rather
than a description of the code.

Blake2bChainSyncHarness drives a live server and is a main rather than a
test, since it needs one. It now goes through SimpleElectrumServerRpc
over a real transport rather than speaking JSON-RPC itself: the earlier
version reimplemented the client, which is why it could not see the
checkBlockHeaders layer. Against electrs on a regtest chain activating at
20: negotiated 1.8, took a 4964 byte response for 40 headers through the
checks, read 19 v1 and 21 v2, and HeaderChainState accepted all 40 under
its linkage, difficulty, proof-of-work and median-time-past rules.
Restoring the fixed stride stops it at checkBlockHeaders.

Blake2bLiveHeadersHarness does the same against the live chain rather than
a made one. Nothing about that data is chosen: the headers come off a fork
peer over p2p, the anchor is the last checkpoint Sparrow already ships for
testnet4 at height 149183, and the difficulty targets, timestamps and
proof of work are whatever the network produced. 600 headers from 149184,
353 v1 and 247 v2, with the first v2 header at 149537, which is the
documented activation height found from the data rather than told to it.
All 600 passed checkBlockHeaders and then HeaderChainState.

202 Sparrow tests pass, with drongo at 542 and lark at 3.
